<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/3rd-century-bce">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Cissa</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/218-bce">218 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-punic-war">Second Punic War</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Cissa was fought in 218 BCE near the town of Cissa, north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarraco">Tarraco</a> in north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, early in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-punic-war">Second Punic War</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gnaeus-cornelius-scipio-calvus">Gnaeus Cornelius Scipio Calvus</a>, who had landed at Emporion, defeated the Carthaginian commander Hanno, left to hold the region north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ebro-river">Ebro</a>, capturing him and his camp. The victory gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> its first firm foothold in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-cissa.jpg" alt="Battle of Cissa" width="320" height="190" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Cristiano64 This file was derived from: Pyrenees topographic map-fr.svg Sting · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:218_AC_-_Iter_Gnaei_Scipionis_in_Hispania.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>In the summer of 218 BCE Hannibal crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ebro-river">Ebro</a> and spent July and August subduing the tribes between the river and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyrenees">Pyrenees</a> at heavy cost, leaving the Greek cities alone. He left a general named Hanno, variously identified as his nephew, his brother or no relation, to hold the conquered land with 10,000 infantry and 1,000 cavalry, watch the pro-Roman Bargusii, guard the link with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hasdrubal-barca">Hasdrubal Barca</a> and keep the heavy baggage left in a camp at Cissa. The Roman expedition to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a> had been delayed when the Boii and Insubres attacked the colonies of Placentia and Cremona and troops were sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cisalpine-gaul">Cisalpine Gaul</a> instead.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> arrived within four to five weeks of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a>'s departure and took Hanno by surprise, as he had marched south to put down a rebellion. He withdrew inland to Cissa and probably raised Iberian levies, perhaps under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indibilis">Indibilis</a>, chieftain of the Ilergetes, while Hasdrubal marched north from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthago-nova">Cartagena</a> with 8,000 infantry and 1,000 cavalry.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The Roman army, strengthened by garrisons and Iberian auxiliaries, may have numbered between 20,000 and 25,000, and outnumbered Hanno about two to one. There were no manoeuvres or ambushes: the armies formed up and fought, and Hanno was beaten relatively easily, losing 6,000 men. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> took his camp, with all the baggage left by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a>, 2,000 more soldiers and Hanno himself, and among the prisoners was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indibilis">Indibilis</a>. They also stormed Cissa, which held no valuable booty.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Hasdrubal arrived too late, but his raiding column caught Roman sailors and marines foraging and reduced the effective Roman fleet in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a> from 60 to 35 ships. Scipio punished the naval officers for lax discipline, shared out the booty and wintered at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarraco">Tarraco</a>, while Hasdrubal garrisoned allied towns south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ebro-river">Ebro</a> and returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthago-nova">Cartagena</a>. The battle cut <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a>'s overland link with Iberia, and he never received reinforcements from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> during the war.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Cissa” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 218 BCE Gnaeus Scipio defeated the Carthaginian commander Hanno near <b>Cissa</b>, a town sometimes identified with Kese, and the Romans established their base at Tarraco.</blockquote><a href="/state/cessetani">Cessetani</a></li></ul></section>
